1 Download the iso https://alpinelinux.org/downloads/ Select Any Variants you like
  Standard
  Forsaken Linux Lite as it was intended. Just enough to get you started. Network connection is required

  Extended
  Most common used packages included. Suitable for routers and servers. Runs from RAM. Includes AMD and Intel microcode updates. 

  Netboot
  Kernel, initramfs and modloop for netboot.

  Raspberry Pi
  First generation Pis including Zero/W (armhf). Pi 2 to Pi 3+ generations (armv7). Pi 3 to Pi 5 generations (aarch64).

  Mini root filesystem
  Minimal root filesystem. For use in containers and minimal chroots.

  Virtual
  Similar to standard. Slimmed down kernel. Optimized for virtual systems. 

  Generic U-Boot
  Has default LTS kernel. Includes the U-Boot bootloader. Tarball. 

  Xen
  Built-in support for Xen Hypervisor. Includes packages targetted at Xen usage. Use for Xen Dom0. 

Usually Standard is the Best Choice

2 Flash the USB Stick or Other Installation Media Like CD DVD
3 Boot to alpine linux media then login as root then type setup-alpine then setup alpine linux first when finished type reboot
4 After install login as root first
5 run apk add nano 
  then nano /etc/os-release
  change

NAME="Alpine Linux"
ID=alpine
VERSION_ID="3.24.2"
PRETTY_NAME="Alpine Linux v3.24"
HOME_URL="https://alpinelinux.org"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports/-/issues"

To

NAME="Forsaken Linux Lite"
ID=forsaken
VERSION_ID="3 (at the speed of light)
PRETTY_NAME="Forsaken Linux Lite 3 (at the speed of light)"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/syslacklinux/Forsaken-Linux-Lite"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://github.com/syslacklinux/Forsaken-Linux/issues"

AFter This CTRL X Y Enter

Then nano /etc/issue

Change 

Welcome to Alpine Linux 3.24
Kernel \r on \m (\l)

To
!etc/os-release
!etc/issue
Welcome to \s \r \m (\l)

After Done CTRL X Y Enter

Then run nano /etc/apk/protected_paths.d/custom.list
then add this
!etc/os-release
!etc/issue

After done exit nano by ctrl x y enter

6 Run rm /etc/motd

7 Bootloader 

For Legacy BIOS
Check and Remount /bootOn many Alpine Linux systems, the boot partition is mounted as read-only by default. Run this command to allow changes
mount -o remount,rw /boot

nano /boot/extlinux.conf
Then Change 
MENU TITLE Alpine/Linux Boot Menu
To
MENU TITLE Forsaken Linux Lite Boot Menu
Change
MENU AUTOBOOT Alpine will be booted automatically in # seconds.
To
MENU AUTOBOOT Forsaken Linux Lite will be booted automatically in # seconds.
On APPEND root Remove quiet
After Done Exit Nano WIth CTRL X Y Enter

If you are running a standard disk install, you are done.However, 
if you are running a diskless mode system (running completely from RAM), you must save the changes to your local backup file so they survive a reboot
lbu commit -d

Remount /boot to Read-Only
mount -o remount,ro /boot

For UEFI
run nano /etc/default/grub
Then change 
GRUB_DISABLE_RECOVERY=true
To
GRUB_DISABLE_RECOVERY=false
Then Remove quiet on GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT
Then add GRUB_DISTRIBUTOR="Forsaken Linux Lite"
Then after this done ctrl x y enter then grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg
If you are on a diskless (RAM) installation, make sure to commit the changes to your local backup
lbu commit -d

8 Reboot the system
